 ## Overview
-Restricted Adjacency Merging is a strategy for merging extracted isosurface components from adjacent spatial blocks in sparse volume rendering [S1]. It solves the problem of inefficiently merging blocks that do not share a true geometric boundary by restricting merges to only those blocks (brothers or nephews) that are adjacent in space [S1]. It sets itself apart from unrestricted merging by enforcing an ancestry-based type check to ensure a common boundary exists, preventing the merging of non-adjacent components [S1].
+Restricted Adjacency Merging is a topological optimization strategy for parallel isosurface extraction from sparse voxel data. It solves the load-balancing problem in hierarchical partitioning by allowing a processed block to merge with a sibling (brother) or a cousin (nephew) if they share a geometric common boundary, rather than restricting merges to immediate siblings only [S1]. This approach reduces the number of final mesh components and communication overhead compared to strict brother-only merging, while avoiding the geometric discontinuities of unrestricted merging [S1]. It differs from [Boundary Point Identification and Merge Simplification](5296-boundary-point-identification-and-merge-simplification.md) by handling the topological eligibility of the merge; the latter handles the subsequent geometric vertex alignment and mesh simplification [S1, 5296].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of extracted isosurface components from leaf nodes of an octree, where each component consists of triangles [S1].
-*   **Output:** A set of merged, simplified isosurface components ready for dumping to disk [S1].
+**Inputs:** An octree structure where nodes are marked as "processed" (extracted) or "unprocessed". Each node has a defined `type` based on its position relative to its father's split.
+**Outputs:** A set of merged blocks, where merged blocks are marked as "completely processed" and their constituent leaves are removed from the active extraction queue.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Node Type:** An enum or integer representing the position of a node relative to its father's split plane: `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `front`, `back` [S1].
-*   **Ancestry Set ($A_{p,n}$):** A set of nodes containing node $n$ and its recursive fathers up to root $p$ [S1].
-
-**Definitions:**
-1.  **Opposite Operator:** Defines the spatial relationship between siblings [S1].
-    $$
-    \begin{aligned}
-    \text{left} &= \text{right}, & \text{right} &= \text{left} \\
-    \text{up} &= \text{down}, & \text{down} &= \text{up} \\
-    \text{front} &= \text{back}, & \text{back} &= \text{front}
-    \end{aligned}
-    $$
-2.  **Ancestry ($A_{p,n}$):** The set of nodes on the path from $n$ to $p$ [S1]. Defined recursively:
-    $$
-    A_{p,n} = \begin{cases} 
-    \{n\} & \text{if } n = p \\
-    \{n\} \cup A_{p,f_n} & \text{else}
-    \end{cases}
-    $$
-    Where $f_n$ is the father of node $n$ [S1].
-3.  **Restricted Adjacency Criterion:** A nephew node $n$ belonging to the tree of root $p_2$ ($T_{p2}$) has a common boundary with block $p_1$ if and only if [S1]:
-    $$
-    \forall n' \in A_{c_{p2},n}, \text{type}(n') \neq \text{type}(p1)
-    $$
-    Where $c_{p2}$ is the child of $p_2$ that is an ancestor of $n$ (or $n$ itself if $n$ is a child of $p_2$) [S1]. Essentially, no ancestor of the nephew (up to the sibling of the target) can have the same type as the target block [S1].
+*   **Node:** Contains geometry data, a `type` field (enum: `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `front`, `back`), and a `status` field (`processed`, `completely_processed`, `unprocessed`).
+*   **Type Definition:** The type is determined by the split direction of the father node. If the father splits along the x-axis, children are `left` and `right`. If along y, `up` and `down`. If along z, `front` and `back` [S1].
+*   **Opposite Operator:** Defined as:
+    *   $left = right$
+    *   $right = left$
+    *   $up = down$
+    *   $down = up$
+    *   $front = back$
+    *   $back = front$ [S1]
 
 **Algorithm Steps:**
-1.  Define node types based on the split direction of the father: left/right (x-cut), up/down (y-cut), front/back (z-cut) [S1].
-2.  Identify candidate merge partners for each unprocessed leaf node: its brother node and any nephew nodes in the subtrees of the brother's siblings [S1].
-3.  Verify adjacency: brothers always share a boundary; for nephews, compute the ancestry set up to the relevant child and verify that no ancestor shares the same type as the target block [S1].
-4.  Merge geometry: identify common points on the shared boundary using the Marching Cubes property that a cell edge generates at most one point, making points on common edges identical [S1].
-5.  Combine triangle lists and simplify the pasted area using error criterion $E_0$ [S1].
-6.  Apply the time lag guard: prevent contraction of an edge $ab$ if the sphere centered on the midpoint of $ab$ with radius $rad(c)$ is not totally included in the merged blocks [S1].
-7.  Mark processed: when two brothers are merged, their father node is considered completely processed [S1].
-8.  Dump finished components to disk [S1].
+1.  **Iterative Traversal:** Traverse the tree to identify processed leaf nodes.
+2.  **Candidate Selection:** For a processed block $p1$, identify potential merge partners. These can be:
+    *   Its brother $p2$ (if $p2$ is also processed).
+    *   A nephew node $n$ belonging to the tree of the brother ($T_{p2}$), provided $n$ is processed and not yet completely processed [S1].
+3.  **Restricted Adjacency Check:** To determine if a nephew $n$ (from the brother's subtree $T_{c_{p2}}$) shares a common boundary with $p1$, compute the ancestry set $A_{c_{p2}, n}$ and verify the type constraint [S1].
+    *   **Ancestry Set Definition:** The ancestry of node $n$ up to root $p$ is defined recursively:
+        $$ A_{p,n} = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } n = p \\ n \cup A_{p,f_n} & \text{else} \end{cases} $$
+        where $f_n$ is the father of $n$ [S1].
+    *   **Boundary Condition:** A nephew $n$ belonging to $T_{c_{p2}}$ has a common boundary with $p1$ if and only if:
+        $$ \forall n' \in A_{c_{p2},n}, \text{type}(n') \neq \text{type}(p1) $$
+        This ensures that no ancestor of the nephew shares the same type (and thus the same split orientation/position relative to the split plane) as the target block $p1$, which would imply they do not share a direct geometric boundary [S1].
+4.  **Merge Execution:** If the condition is met, merge $p1$ and $n$ (or $p1$ and its brother). The resulting merged node is marked as `completely_processed`.
+5.  **Completion:** When two brothers are merged, their father node is considered completely processed [S1].
 
