 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Numerical Inaccuracy:** Common points on shared boundaries cannot be identified by simple coordinate comparison [S1].
-*   **Ancestry Check Failure:** If the ancestry check is not performed correctly, non-adjacent blocks may be merged, leading to topological errors [S1].
-*   **Processing Order:** The algorithm requires a specific traversal order (bottom-up) to ensure that when a merge happens, the components are ready [S1].
+*   **Ancestry Check Failure:** If any ancestor of the nephew shares the same type as the target block, they do not share a common boundary, and merging is forbidden [S1].
+*   **Processing Order:** The algorithm relies on nodes being marked `completely_processed` to prevent re-merging. If a node is merged, it must be excluded from further merge candidates [S1].
+*   **Numerical Inaccuracy:** While the topological merge is defined by tree structure, the actual geometric merging requires handling vertex coordinates. Simple coordinate comparison is insufficient due to numerical inaccuracies in isosurface extraction [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Boundary Point Identification and Merge Simplification
-This variant details the geometric handling of merged surfaces [S1]. It identifies common points extracted in each block lying on their shared boundary [S1]. It merges points belonging to common cell edges, leveraging the property that a cell edge generates only one or zero points, making points on common edges identical [S1]. It simplifies the pasted area using the same error criterion $E_0$ as for surface extraction [S1]. It prevents edge contraction $ab$ during merge simplification as long as the sphere centered on the middle of $ab$ with radius $rad(c)$ is not totally included in the merged blocks [S1]. Finally, it dumps finished components to disk [S1].
+### [Boundary Point Identification and Merge Simplification](5296-boundary-point-identification-and-merge-simplification.md)
+Handles the geometric vertex alignment and mesh simplification constraints after the topological merge eligibility has been determined by Restricted Adjacency Merging.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces the number of triangles significantly by merging adjacent components [S1]. Avoids waiting for slower blocks by allowing selective merging with nephews [S1]. Improves boundary triangulation quality by applying the time lag method near block boundaries during simplification [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful implementation of the ancestry check [S1]. Merging is restricted, so not all adjacent components may be merged if they do not meet the strict adjacency criteria [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When extracting isosurfaces from large volumes partitioned into blocks, especially when parallel extraction leads to fragmented components that need to be recombined [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The ancestry check requires traversing up the tree from the nephew to the common ancestor. In the worst case, this is $O(h)$ where $h$ is the tree height. The number of potential merge candidates is limited by the local neighborhood in the octree.
+*   **Performance:** Allows merging of non-sibling components, reducing the total number of mesh parts and potentially improving cache coherence and reducing post-processing overhead [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful management of node states (`processed` vs `completely_processed`) to avoid invalid merges. The geometric merging step (not covered here) must handle the boundary points identified by this topological step [S1, 5296].
 
 ## Background
-Restricted Adjacency Merging was introduced in the context of parallel extraction and simplification of large isosurfaces using an extended tandem algorithm [S1]. It addresses the issue of non-uniform distribution of iso-surface components in sub-blocks, which can lead to load imbalance and inefficient merging if all brothers are forced to merge [S1].
+Restricted Adjacency Merging was introduced in the context of parallel extraction and simplification of large isosurfaces using an extended tandem algorithm. It addresses the issue where iso-surface components are not uniformly distributed, causing load imbalance if only brothers are merged [S1]. The method was proposed to allow merging with nephews under strict topological constraints to maintain geometric continuity [S1].
 
